  "body": "Part of #1490.\n\nThe rest generator currently trusts an entered previous tool. It assumes that tool cut the pocket to the requested depth.\n\nAdd a previous-operations mode that derives remaining material from the stock those operations actually leave.\n\nDesign decision:\n- Keep the entered-tool mode explicit. Add a separate previous-operations mode through an undoable command, the project file, and MCP.\n- Read only earlier operations in the same setup, in their actual order. Reuse the established carried-stock infrastructure.\n- Require current operation paths and compatible setup coordinates. Refuse missing, stale, or unsupported stock by name, or mark the result incomplete.\n- Read remaining stock at every requested cut depth. A shallow roughing pass does not prove that the lower floor is clear.\n- Include tool shape, wall and floor allowances, and the supported simulation resolution. Unknown cells remain occupied.\n- Pass a conservative remaining-material region to the motion generator. Do not infer cleared volume from the previous tool diameter alone.\n- Invalidate the result when an earlier operation changes, moves, is suppressed, or changes its tool or depth.\n- Keep the current stock backend. Do not combine this feature with the field-engine migration in #586 or #637.\n\nTests:\n- Compare the rest region with independently simulated earlier operations.\n- Test mixed previous tool sizes, bull-nose tools, partial depths, wall allowances, islands, and disconnected cuts.\n- Test missing paths, stale paths, operation reordering, changed tools, and unsupported stock frames.\n- Prove the plan never removes material from its rest estimate unless the earlier cuts prove that material absent.\n- Prove command undo, project save and reopen, MCP editing, and dependency invalidation.\n- Remove each depth and stock-validity guard separately. Each mutation must fail its pinning test.\n\nDone means: previous-operations mode uses conservative stock at each depth, passes these tests, and never guesses when stock cannot be established.\n\nRelated: #586 and #637 migrate the stock representation. This issue consumes the current representation for 2D rest machining.\n\nLiterature: M. Held, On the Computational Geometry of Pocket Machining, LNCS 500 (1991), https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/3-540-54103-9. Z. Yao, S. K. Gupta and D. S. Nau, Algorithms for selecting cutters in multi-part milling problems, Computer-Aided Design 35 (2003), https://www.cs.umd.edu/~nau/papers/yao2003algorithms.pdf.\n\nThese sources describe pocket geometry and cutter coverage. The per-depth stock and invalidation contract above is this project's decision. Do not copy another CAM product's code or output.\n",
